.SH NAME
.PP
auto-change-group-ownership - Change group ownership of select files

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-change-group-ownership [--silent] username|uid old-groupname|gid new-groupname|gid directory [directory ...]
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
--silent            Option to suppress output of filenames processed
username|uid        Username or UID of files/directories to be changed
old-groupname|gid   Groupname or GID of files/directories to be changed
new-groupname|gid   New groupname or GID for discovered files/directories
directory           Top-level directory under which to search
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-change-group-ownership
command changes the group ownership of select files/directories under one or
more top-level directories.  It searches under the specified directories using
find(1) for files/direcotries owned by the given user and old group, and changes
the group ownership to the new group.

This is a convenience script allowing users to avoid complicated
and error-prone find(1) commands.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-change-group-ownership --silent joeuser joeuser projectX /collab/projectX
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/group
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-change-ownership(8), chgrp(1)

